Michael remembers exactly what went  through his head the day he was  diagnosed with HIV it was in January  last year he knew his life had changed  forever you know not only is it my first  thought was would I still be able to  find work oh no I'm positive I had this  idea of jumping off a bridge  oh my god I need to die oh my god I need  to die  Michael is 21 and one of some 35,000  people known to live with HIV in the  Philippines he fears being stigmatized  so he asked us not to show his face as a  teenager he sold sex to other men often  without using a condom knowledge about  HIV and how the virus is transmitted is  still patchy among many Filipinos the  ignorance leads not only to higher  infection rates but also feeds fear and  rejection even in Michael's family so  every day when I got home every day when  I came home my mom would separate my  plates and cutlery from those of the  others she said we needed to keep them  apart I always had the feeling they  found me repulsive after years of  playing down how severely HIV AIDS is  impacting young men like Michael the  government now seems to be changing  course it's at high schools like this  one here in Manila where the Philippine  government wants to start tackling the  HIV epidemic for example by offering  better sex education to teenagers a  proposal that has sparked fierce  criticism from parents conservative  lawmakers but also the church in this  deeply Catholic country critics have  already forced officials to scrap the  idea of making condoms available at  schools they're afraid it would  encourage children to become promiscuous  conservatives say sex education doesn't  belong in the classroom the first  teachers of the children are their  parents  for heaven's sake the parents are not  only supposed to give them food board  and lodging but also to teach them value  system behavioral patterns etc etc to be  good members of society and among other  things is this matter of sexual  education it belongs primarily by right  and by duty to the parish themselves to  do this it's not an approach Michael  things can work neither parents nor  teenagers feel comfortable talking about  sex he himself has become a peer  educator he wants to spare other young  people his fate megumi say everything  starts with knowing once people have  adequate knowledge the right information  about hiv/aids teen pregnancy condoms  and other things you'll be armed your  knowledge will be your shield and your  sword that way you can protect yourself  and at the same time protect other young  people especially those who are close to  you Michael knows he's facing an uphill  battle but for him every HIV case he can  prevent by sharing what he's had to  learn the hard way is a victory
 

